-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Version 5.1, July 12, 2019

5/22/19  [EDGcpfe/20917,EDGcpfe/20948]
C++20: Coroutines

A stub implementation for the C++20 coroutines header has been provided in
coroutine.stdh.  There is not yet any library support for coroutines, as
coroutines are not yet able to be lowered.  This header will, however, allow
for compilation of code that uses coroutines.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Version 5.0, August 10, 2018

9/25/17  [EDGcpfe/10590,EDGcpfe/16574,EDGcpfe/17582]
Implementation of some standard library utilities; new edg_internal_defines
header file

The run time library now implements std::get_terminate, std::get_new_handler,
and std::uncaught_exceptions.

Additionally, a new header file, edg_internal_defines.stdh, has been added to
introduce macros used to conceal differences in C++ versions when compiling
the exceptions.stdh and new.stdh files.  Those files have been updated to use
the new macros.


9/25/17  [EDGcpfe/18806]
Microsoft compatibility: exception specifications for allocation/deallocation
functions

C++17 removes dynamic exception specifications (those using the "throw"
keyword).  The new.stdh file previously determined whether to use the newer
"noexcept" forms based on the value of the __cplusplus predefined macro.
However, the Microsoft compiler always sets __cplusplus to reflect C++98,
resulting in incorrect exception specifications when compiling in Microsoft
C++17 mode.  The new.stdh file has now been changed to use the value of the
Microsoft-specific predefined macro _NOEXCEPT_TYPES_SUPPORTED to determine
which form of exception specification to use in Microsoft-mode
compilations.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Version 4.14, September 12, 2017

8/24/17  [EDGcpfe/17696]
C++17: overaligned storage allocation

The new.stdh file has been modified to define std::align_val_t and to
declare the additional overloaded operators to support allocation and
freeing of overaligned data types, as described in Committee document
P0035R4.  These declarations are only enabled when
__STDCPP_DEFAULT_NEW_ALIGNMENT__ is defined, indicating a C++17
compilation.

