-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Version 2.34, December 18, 1996

11/19/96 Placement delete conditionally compiled

The placement delete declarations in new.h are now guarded by an #ifdef
of __PLACEMENT_DELETE, which is defined by the front end when placement
delete is enabled.

10/15/96 Placement delete enabled

The placement delete declarations in new.h have been enabled.  They were
added in version 2.31, but were disabled because the front end did not
yet support placement delete or overloaded delete operators.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Version 2.33, Octover 2, 1996

9/25/96 uncaught_exception has been added to exception.h

The routine std::uncaught_exception that is specified by the Working
Paper has been added to exception.h.

9/19/96 Exception specifications are now provided for operator new

Exception specifications, as specified by the WP, are now provided on
the declarations of operator new and placement operator new in the
new.h header file.

8/13/96 signal.h update for C vs. C++ routine linkage language changes

signal.h has been updated to make sure that the function types declared/used
have C linkage instead of C++ linkage.  This is important now that C and
C++ linkage are incompatible according to the Working Paper.

7/22/96 Use standard name for the new handler type

In new.h, __new_handler was changed to new_handler as specified in the
working paper.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Version 2.32, June 28, 1996

5/6/96  Revised nothrow new implementation

X3J16/WG21 changed std::nothrow from a type to an object because the
disambiguation rules made the use of the type difficult.  For example,

	new (std::nothrow()) T;

was interpreted as allocating an object whose type was "function taking
no argument and returning a std::nothrow", instead of the desired
interpretation of passing an std::nothrow object to the new routine.

We now define std::nothrow as an object of type std::nothrow_t.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Version 2.31, January 19, 1996

1/10/96  Added iso646.h header file

The iso646.h header has been added.  It defines macros for the alternative
spellings (e.g., bitand, bitor) for certain operators as specified in
Normative Addendum 1 of the ISO C standard.

12/28/95 Added placement array new and delete and nothrow new routines

The placement version of array new and delete have been added to new.h, as
have the nothrow versions of new and array new.  The placement delete
routines, although present, have been disabled because the front end
does not yet support overloaded operator delete routines, nor calling
the placement delete routines in the EH mechanism.

12/1/95  Remove include of iostream.h from assert.h

The assert.h include file was changed to use stdio.h instead of iostream.h.
This change was made to reduce the number of header files that are pulled
in by assert.h.  Strictly speaking, assert.h should not include anything
else.  A "real" library will need to do assert.h differently.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Version 2.30, October 10, 1995

9/1/95   Changes for namespaces

typeinfo.h, stdexcept.h, new.h, and exception.h have been changed to
put standard types in namespace std, but only if the front-end flag
RUNTIME_USES_NAMESPACES is set to TRUE.

When the runtime uses namespaces, the front end will define a
preprocessing flag (named __EDG_IMPLICIT_USING_STD by default) that
will be TRUE if the --using_std option was specified when the front
end was invoked.  The runtime tests this flag and does a "using
namespace std" directive when it is set.
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
Version 2.29, May 24, 1995

5/4/95   C++ standard header files: typeinfo.h, exception.h, stdexcept.h

typeinfo.h and stdexcept.h have been added.  exception.h has been
modified to reflect changes in the working paper.  The runtime library
source depends on the class definitions specified in these files.
Changes to these files will probably require corresponding changes
in the runtime library and vice-versa.

